<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{.Title}}</title>
<meta name="description" content="Every figure on this page is traced to a page of the City of Livermore's published budget, and what we inferred is marked as inferred.">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
<script>
  // Stamp the saved theme before first paint. Inline and tiny on purpose: a
  // deferred script would repaint the page in the wrong mode first.
  try {
    var t = localStorage.getItem("fisc-theme");
    if (t === "light" || t === "dark") document.documentElement.dataset.theme = t;
  } catch (e) { /* private mode: fall back to the OS setting */ }
</script>
</head>
<body>

<div class="wip-banner" role="status">⚠️ Work in progress — early preview, coverage is incomplete.</div>

<header class="page-head">
  <div>
    <h1>Where Livermore's money comes from, and where it goes</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The <span id="lede-year">{{.FiscalYearLabel}} {{.Basis}}</span> budget, all funds, gross — read out of the city's
      published schedule and traced back to it. Figures the city printed are drawn solid;
      the two things we inferred are drawn dashed and labelled.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="head-controls">
    {{- if gt (len .Years) 1}}
    <!-- A radio group, not a <select>: two options that swap the whole page are
         a choice between peers, and a radio group says which one you are
         looking at without being opened.

         IT SHIPS DISABLED and app.js enables it. Switching year is entirely a
         client behaviour — it refetches a document and repaints — so with
         JavaScript off the control could be moved and nothing would follow it:
         the reader would select FY2026-27 and go on reading FY2025-26's
         figures, with the styling agreeing with them. A control that lies is
         worse than one that is absent, and the year on show is still stated in
         the lede either way. -->
    <fieldset class="year-toggle" id="year-toggle" disabled>
      <legend>Budget year</legend>
      {{- range $i, $y := .Years}}
      <input type="radio" name="fisc-year" id="year-{{$y.Year}}" value="{{$y.Stem}}"
             {{if eq $i 0}}checked{{end}}>
      <label for="year-{{$y.Year}}">{{$y.Label}}</label>
      {{- end}}
    </fieldset>
    {{- end}}
    <button type="button" id="theme-toggle" aria-pressed="false">Dark mode</button>
  </div>
</header>

<main>

<section class="figures" id="figures" aria-label="Headline figures">
  <div class="tile hero">
    <div class="label">{{.Hero.Label}}</div>
    <div class="value">{{.Hero.Value}}</div>
    <div class="note">{{.Hero.Note}}</div>
  </div>
  {{- range .Figures}}
  <div class="tile{{if .Kind}} {{.Kind}}{{end}}">
    <div class="label">{{.Label}}</div>
    <div class="value">{{.Value}}</div>
    <div class="note">{{.Note}}</div>
  </div>
  {{- end}}
</section>

<h2>The flow</h2>
<p class="lede">
  Revenue categories on the left, the city's six fund groups in the middle, what the money
  is spent on at the right. Transfers between funds and movements of accumulated fund
  balance are drawn as their own endpoints, because they are not money crossing the city's
  boundary — counting them as spending is exactly the error the headline avoids.
</p>

<div class="card">
  <div class="controls">
    <div class="legend" id="legend" aria-label="Fund groups"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="legend" aria-label="How to read the marks">
    <span><span class="key published" aria-hidden="true"></span> printed by the city</span>
    <span><span class="key derived" aria-hidden="true"></span> ◇ our inference (dashed)</span>
    <span class="subtle">Hover, tap or tab to a flow for its amount, fact ids and sources.
      Click a node or a fund swatch to follow only that money; Escape puts the rest back.</span>
  </div>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <!-- role="group", not "img": an img is a leaf, and the marks inside carry
         tabindex and aria-label so a keyboard reader can walk the flows. Under
         role="img" they are reachable but announced as nothing, which the
         "tab to a flow" instruction below would then be lying about. -->
    <svg class="sankey" id="chart" role="group" aria-labelledby="chart-title chart-desc">
      <title id="chart-title">Sankey diagram of the {{.FiscalYearLabel}} {{.Basis}} budget</title>
      <desc id="chart-desc">
        Revenue categories flow into six fund groups, which flow into object categories,
        transfers out and fund balance. The same figures are listed in the table below.
      </desc>
    </svg>
    <div class="tooltip" id="tooltip" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
  </div>
  <div class="detail" id="detail" aria-live="polite">
    <p class="subtle">Select a flow or a node to pin its provenance here.</p>
  </div>
  <noscript>
    <p>
      This page draws its chart in the browser from
      <a href="{{.PrimaryPath}}">{{.PrimaryPath}}</a>. With JavaScript off, that file and the
      figures above are still the whole story: every amount, fact id and source page is in it.
    </p>
  </noscript>
</div>

<h2>What we inferred</h2>
<p class="lede">
  A figure the city printed and a classification we made are different things, so they are
  never drawn alike. Everything listed here is ours, with the reasoning and the row it came
  from.
</p>
<ul class="derived-list" id="derived-list">
  <li class="subtle">Loading from {{.PrimaryPath}}…</li>
</ul>

<h2>Caveats</h2>
<p class="lede">
  These are the reasons this chart will not reconcile row-for-row against the printed
  schedule. They ship with the data, not in a footnote.
</p>
<ul class="caveats" id="caveats">
  {{- range .Caveats}}
  <li>{{.}}</li>
  {{- end}}
</ul>

<h2>Every flow, as a table</h2>
<p class="lede">
  The chart's twin. Same numbers, no colour, no hovering — <span id="counts-line">{{.Links}} flows between
  {{.Nodes}} nodes, from {{.Facts}} facts</span>.
</p>
<details id="table-view" open>
  <summary>Show the table</summary>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table id="flow-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">From</th>
          <th scope="col">To</th>
          <th scope="col">Amount</th>
          <th scope="col">Kind</th>
          <th scope="col">Basis</th>
          <th scope="col">Fact ids</th>
          <th scope="col">Source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody></tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</details>

</main>

<footer>
  <h3>Sources</h3>
  <ul>
    {{- range .Sources}}
    <li>
      {{if .PDFURL}}<a href="{{.PDFURL}}">{{.Title}}</a>{{else}}{{.Title}}{{end}}{{if .Publisher}}, {{.Publisher}}{{end}}
      {{- if .Pages}} —
      {{- range $i, $p := .Pages}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}
        <a href="{{$p.PDFURL}}">p{{$p.Number}} (PDF)</a> /
        <a href="{{$p.TextURL}}">extracted text</a>
      {{- end}}{{end}}
    </li>
    {{- end}}
  </ul>
  <p class="subtle">
    Scope {{.Scope}}, basis {{.Basis}}. Projection: {{.ProjectionBy}}. Packaged by {{.ExportedBy}}.
    <!-- The two sentences are separate because the claims are. The scope and
         basis above are THIS PAGE'S, read out of the document it is drawn from;
         the list below is every document the site ships, and since the site
         gained a second schedule they are no longer the same set. Running them
         together would say the revenue trends are all-funds-gross adopted
         FY2025-26, which is four wrong things about one file. -->
    This chart is drawn from <a href="{{.PrimaryPath}}">{{.PrimaryPath}}</a>.
    Every data file the site publishes, each fetchable on its own:
    {{- range $i, $p := .Projections}}{{if $i}},{{end}} <a href="{{$p.Path}}">{{$p.Path}}</a>{{end}}.
  </p>
</footer>

<script src="vendor/d3.min.js"></script>
<script src="vendor/d3-sankey.min.js"></script>
<script>window.FISC_CONFIG = {{.ConfigJSON}};</script>
<script src="app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
